  "stdout": "# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid development of large language models (LLMs) has enabled the emergence of autonomous code agents that can generate, debug, and execute code with minimal human intervention. While these code agents offer significant productivity benefits, they also introduce novel security and safety concerns that demand rigorous evaluation. This paper provides a comprehensive survey of recent advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies for assessing their trustworthiness. We examine the landscape of code agent architectures, their capabilities, and associated risks including vulnerability generation, unauthorized system access, and code execution exploits. We also review red-teaming approaches specifically designed to evaluate code agents, including adversarial prompt engineering, automated test generation, and benchmark-based evaluations. Furthermore, we discuss the lessons learned from trustworthiness assessments of general-purpose language models and their applicability to the code agent domain. Our analysis reveals critical gaps in current evaluation frameworks and proposes directions for future research in building safer and more reliable code agents.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe past few years have witnessed an unprecedented surge in the capabilities of large language models (LLMs), leading to the development of autonomous software agents capable of generating, modifying, and executing code with increasing sophistication. Code agents represent a paradigm shift in software development, offering the promise of accelerated development cycles, automated testing, and intelligent code review. However, with these advancements come significant security and safety concerns that must be thoroughly understood and mitigated.\n\nCode agents operate in diverse and often sensitive environments, from development tools to production systems. When equipped with code execution capabilities, these agents can potentially introduce vulnerabilities, execute malicious payloads, or compromise system security. The trustworthiness of such agents depends on multiple factors, including their ability to follow security best practices, avoid generating exploitable code, and resist adversarial manipulation.\n\nRecent work has highlighted the importance of comprehensive trustworthiness assessments for large language models. The DecodingTrust framework provides a comprehensive assessment of trustworthiness in GPT models, covering dimensions such as bias, toxicity, stereotypes, misinformation, factuality, hidden biases, and malicious uses [1]. While this work focuses on general-purpose models, its methodology offers valuable insights for evaluating the trustworthiness of code agents specifically.\n\nSimultaneously, the field of red-teaming has emerged as a critical practice for evaluating the robustness and safety of AI systems. Red-teaming involves systematically attempting to elicit undesirable or unsafe behaviors from models through carefully crafted prompts and inputs. For code agents, red-teaming must address unique challenges such as prompt injection, code injection attacks, sandbox escape attempts, and the generation of security vulnerabilities.\n\nThis paper makes the following contributions:\n\n1. We provide a comprehensive overview of code agent architectures and their evolving capabilities.\n2. We survey recent advances in red-teaming methodologies for code agents and related systems.\n3. We analyze the trustworthiness dimensions relevant to code agents, drawing on established frameworks for evaluating language models.\n4. We identify key challenges and open research directions in the field.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Trustworthiness Assessment in Language Models\n\nThe trustworthiness of large language models has become a critical area of research as these systems are increasingly deployed in real-world applications. DecodingTrust [1] presents a comprehensive assessment framework that evaluates GPT models across multiple trustworthiness dimensions, including bias and toxicity, stereotypes, misinformation and factuality, hidden biases, and susceptibility to malicious uses. The authors demonstrate that even state-of-the-art models can exhibit significant trustworthiness issues, particularly when subjected to adversarial prompts designed to elicit harmful outputs.\n\nThe DecodingTrust framework provides valuable methodological foundations for evaluating code agents. While code agents have specialized capabilities for software development, they inherit the fundamental trustworthiness challenges of their underlying language model foundations. Issues such as generating biased code, producing misleading documentation, or inadvertently creating security vulnerabilities must be systematically assessed.\n\n### 2.2 Deep Learning Foundations\n\nThe remarkable progress in natural language processing that enables modern language models and code agents builds upon foundational advances in deep learning architecture. Deep Residual Learning [2], which introduced the residual learning framework, revolutionized training of deep neural networks and enabled the construction of substantially deeper architectures. This breakthrough laid the groundwork for the extremely deep transformer models that power contemporary code agents. The residual learning paradigm has become a fundamental building block in modern deep learning, enabling the stable training of networks with hundreds or even thousands of layers.\n\n### 2.3 Code Agents\n\nThe development of code agents has evolved from simple code completion tools to sophisticated autonomous agents capable of complex software development tasks. Early code agents focused on autocomplete and syntax correction, often powered by specialized models trained on large code corpora. Recent advances have integrated these capabilities with language understanding, enabling agents to reason about code, generate multi-file projects, and interact with development environments.\n\nModern code agents typically incorporate several key components: (1) code-aware language models trained on diverse code repositories; (2) tool-use capabilities enabling interaction with compilers, debuggers, and other development tools; (3) planning and reasoning mechanisms for decomposing complex tasks; and (4) memory systems for maintaining context across development sessions. These capabilities enable code agents to tackle increasingly complex software engineering tasks but also expand the attack surface for potential misuse.\n\n### 2.4 Red-teaming and Security Evaluation\n\nRed-teaming has become an essential practice for evaluating the safety and robustness of AI systems. Traditional red-teaming approaches for language models involve attempting to elicit undesirable outputs such as hate speech, misinformation, or instructions for harmful activities. For code agents, red-teaming must extend to evaluating security-specific vulnerabilities.\n\nRecent work has developed specialized benchmarks and methodologies for red-teaming code agents. These include benchmarks for testing vulnerability generation, sandbox escape capabilities, and susceptibility to adversarial prompts designed to trick the agent into executing harmful code. Automated red-teaming approaches leverage meta-agents that systematically explore the input space to discover vulnerabilities in code agents.\n\n## 3. Trustworthiness Dimensions for Code Agents\n\nBuilding on the comprehensive framework established for general language models, we identify the following trustworthiness dimensions particularly relevant to code agents:\n---\n\nDrawing on established frameworks for evaluating language model trustworthiness, such as DecodingTrust, we have identified key dimensions for assessing code agent reliability. The foundational advances in deep learning, exemplified by work such as Deep Residual Learning, have enabled the sophisticated architectures that power modern code agents. As we continue to develop and deploy these systems, rigorous red-teaming and trustworthiness assessment must remain central to the development process.\n\nSeveral important research directions remain open:\n\n1. **Standardized Evaluation Frameworks**: The field would benefit from widely adopted benchmarks and evaluation standards for code agent trustworthiness, analogous to established practices in machine learning.\n\n2. **Adversarial Robustness**: Developing code agents that are robust to adversarial attacks and prompt injection requires both defensive techniques and comprehensive vulnerability assessments.\n\n3. **Human-Agent Collaboration**: Understanding how to effectively pair code agents with human developers, ensuring appropriate oversight while maximizing productivity, remains an important research challenge.\n\n4. **Long-term Impact**: As code agents become more capable and autonomous, understanding their long-term impact on software development practices, security postures, and developer workflows requires ongoing research and monitoring.\n\nBy addressing these challenges through collaborative research and systematic evaluation, we can work toward code agents that are not only powerful and productive but also trustworthy and safe for widespread deployment.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei et al., \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models,\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[2] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, Jian Sun, \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition,\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\n",
